"import boto3\nimport requests\nfrom urllib3.util import SSLContext\nimport ssl\n\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    s3 = boto3.client('s3')\n    s3.put_object(Bucket='horserace-dx', Key='/raw/a.html', Body='testtext')\n    return event\n\n!pip install requests -t ./python --no-user\n!pip install pandas -t ./python --no-user\n!pip install beautifulsoup4 -t ./python --no-user\n!pip install urllib3==1.26.15 -t ./python --no-user\n!pip install PyOpenSSL -t ./python --no-userassistant\n\nThe error you're encountering is due to a change in the way urllib3 handles SSL contexts in Python 3.7 and later versions. In earlier versions of Python, the `DEFAULT_CIPHERS` setting was used to specify the SSL ciphers to use. However, starting from Python 3.7, this setting has been deprecated and removed.\n\nTo resolve this issue, you need to manually create an SSL context and specify the ciphers you want to use. You can do this by importing the `SSLContext` class from `urllib3.util.ssl_` and creating a new instance with the desired ciphers.\n\nThe code snippet below shows how you can modify your `lambda_handler` function to create an SSL context and use it with the `requests` library:\n\n```python\nimport boto3\nimport requests\nfrom urllib3.util import SSLContext\nimport ssl\n\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    s3 = boto3.client('s3')\n    # Create an SSL context with the desired ciphers\n    context = SSLContext(ssl.create_default_context()).context\n    \n    # Use the SSL context with the requests library\n    response = requests.get('https://example.com', verify=False, context=context)\n    \n    # Upload the data to S3\n    s3.put_object(Bucket='horserace-dx', Key='/raw/a.html', Body=response.text)\n    \n    return event\n```\n\nPlease note that you need to replace `'https://example.com'` with the actual URL of the website you want to scrape.\n\nAlso, keep in mind that using `verify=False` can make your application vulnerable to man-in-the-middle attacks. Make sure to only use this option if you're absolutely sure that the website you're connecting to is trustworthy."
