n    print(\"$ ldconfig -N -v $(sed 's/:/ /' <<< $LD_LIBRARY_PATH) 2>/dev/null | grep libcudnn_ops_train.so\")\n\n    print(\"$ ldconfig -N -v $(sed 's/:/ /' <<< $LD_LIBRARY_PATH) 2>/dev/null | grep libcudnn_ops_infer.so\")\n\n# Main function\ndef main():\n    check_cuda_drivers()\n    check_cudnn()\n    check_cuda()\n    print(f'\\nTensorflow version = {tf.__version__}')\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```"
"import functools\nimport time\n\n@functools.cache\ndef ack(x, y):\n    for i in range(x, 0, -1):\n        y = ack(i, y - 1) if y else 1\n    return y + 1\n\nprint(ack(3, 4))assistant\n\nThis code uses the `@functools.cache` decorator to memoize the results of the `ack` function. This means that if the function is called with the same arguments again, it will return the cached result instead of recalculating it. \n\nHowever, as the original code pointed out, memoization doesn't help with different arguments. Also, due to the nature of the Ackermann function, it's not possible to optimize its time complexity without fundamentally changing its definition. The Ackermann function grows extremely rapidly, and its recursive nature makes it difficult to optimize."
"import timeit\n\nfor _ in range(3):\n    t1 = timeit(lambda: bytearray(1000000)[0], 'b = bytearray(1000000)')\n    t2 = timeit(lambda: del bytearray(1000000)[0], 'b = bytearray(1000000)')\n    print(t1 / t2)assistant\n\nThe reason `b.pop(0)` is much slower than `del b[0]` is due to the way Python handles bytearray objects. When you use `pop(0)`, Python creates a new bytearray object and copies the first element to it, which involves allocating new memory and performing a copy operation. This is a slower operation compared to deleting the first element directly from the existing bytearray object, which only requires updating the internal pointers.\n\nThe `del` statement, on the other hand, modifies the bytearray object in-place, without creating a new object or copying data. This is why `del b[0]` is faster than `b.pop(0)`."
