"```python\ndef explain_gcc_behavior():\n    # The behavior is due to the order of operations in C/C++ and how the compiler interprets the &amp;&amp; operator.\n    # In the first case, the condition is g_ptrArray[idx] != nullptr && idx < ARRAY_LENGTH.\n    # The compiler evaluates the right operand of the &amp;&amp; operator first, which is idx < ARRAY_LENGTH.\n    # This condition is not checked by the compiler because the compiler only checks for nullptr.\n    # In the second case, the condition is g_ptrArray[idx] != nullptr &&& idx < ARRAY_LENGTH.\n    # The &amp;&amp; operator is not present in the first operand, so the compiler evaluates the left operand.\n    # However, the left operand is not a condition, but an assignment.\n    # Therefore, the &amp;&amp; operator is not evaluated and the condition is not checked.\n\n    return \"The compiler ignores the idx < ARRAY_LENGTH condition because it is evaluated after the &amp;&amp; operator, which only checks for nullptr.\"\n\nprint(explain_gcc_behavior())\n```"
"```python\ndef test_multidimensional_array_access():\n    arr = [[[[] for _ in range(4)] for _ in range(4)] for _ in range(4)]\n    i, j, k, l = 0, 0, 0, 0\n\n    def a():\n        nonlocal i\n        i += 1\n        return i\n\n    def b():\n        nonlocal j\n        j += 1\n        return j\n\n    def c():\n        nonlocal k\n        k += 1\n        return k\n\n    def d():\n        nonlocal l\n        l += 1\n        return l\n\n    arr[a()][b()][c()][d()] = 1\n\n    print(\"a\", end=' ')\n    arr[1][b()][c()][d()] = 1\n    print(\"b\", end=' ')\n    arr[1][1][c()][d()] = 1\n    print(\"c\", end=' ')\n    arr[1][1][1][d()] = 1\n    print(\"d\", end=' ')\n    arr[1][1][1][1] = 1\n\ntest_multidimensional_array_access()\n```"
