"```cpp\ntemplate <class key_type, class value_type, int N>\nclass flat_map {\nprivate:\n    struct pair {\n        key_type key;\n        value_type value;\n    };\n\n    const pair elements[N];\n\npublic:\n    explicit constexpr flat_map(const pair (&arr)[N]) noexcept\n        : elements(arr) {}\n\n    [[nodiscard]] constexpr value_type operator[](const key_type key) const {\n        for (const pair &elem : elements)\n            if (elem.key == key)\n                return elem.value;\n        throw std::out_of_range(\"Key not found\");\n    }\n};\n\nconstexpr flat_map<int, char, 3> m = {\n    { 4, 'a' }, { -1, 'b' }, { 42, 'c' }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    return m[4]; // 97 == 'a'\n}\n```"
"```cpp\n#include <utility>\n\nint main() {\n    int x[6];\n    int y[6];\n    std::pair<int*, int*> a(x, y);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nThis code is allowed because `std::pair` in C++ takes two arguments of the same type, not different types. The syntax `std::pair<int[6], int[6]>` is not valid in C++. However, you can create an array of pairs instead:\n\n```cpp\n#include <utility>\n#include <vector>\n\nint main() {\n    int x[6];\n    int y[6];\n    std::vector<std::pair<int, int>> a(x, x+6);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `a` is a vector of pairs, where each pair contains an element from `x` and an element from `y`."
