"def can_form_palindrome(arr):\n    n = len(arr)\n    for i in range(n - 1):\n        count = {}\n        for j in range(i, n):\n            temp = arr[j]\n            for char in temp:\n                if char in count:\n                    count[char] += 1\n                else:\n                    count[char] = 1\n            if j > i:\n                temp = arr[i]\n                for char in temp:\n                    if char in count:\n                        count[char] -= 1\n                    else:\n                        return False\n    for char in count:\n        if count[char] % 2 != 0:\n            return False\n    return True\n\narr = [\"aa\", \"bab\", \"cde\", \"aba\", \"ab\"]\nprint(can_form_palindrome(arr))assistant\n\nThis function works by iterating over all pairs of consecutive elements in the array. For each pair, it counts the occurrences of each character in both elements. If the characters are different, it moves the rightmost character of the first element to the leftmost position in the second element and vice versa. If the characters are the same, it does nothing. After processing all pairs, it checks if the count of each character is even. If any character has an odd count, it means that character cannot be moved to make the array palindromic, so the function returns False. If all characters have even counts, the function returns True."
