"```python\nclass Blog(models.Model):\n    _id = models.ObjectIdField()\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=100, db_column='Name')\n    tagline = models.TextField()\n\nclass Entry(models.Model):\n    _id = models.ObjectIdField()\n    blog = models.EmbeddedField(\n        model_container=Blog,\n        db_column='blog__name',\n        db_index=True\n    )\n```\n\nThe issue arises because the EmbeddedField's model field name is the same as the column name in the database. To resolve this, you can specify a custom column name using the `db_column` parameter. In this case, we're setting it to `'blog__name'`, which is the camelCase equivalent of `'name'` in snake_case.\n\nAdditionally, we're setting `db_index=True` to maintain data consistency between the model and database. This is because the EmbeddedField's model field name will be used as an index in the database, and we want to ensure it matches the snake_case convention."
