"### Microservices Architecture with Django\n\n#### Overview\n\nThis example demonstrates a basic microservices architecture with Django as the backend API service and React/Angular frontend.\n\n#### Project Structure\n\n```python\nproject/\n    django_app/\n        __init__.py\n        settings.py\n        urls.py\n        admin.py\n        models.py\n        views.py\n    react_app/\n        __init__.py\n        app.js\n    db/\n        __init__.py\n        settings.py\n        models.py\n    go_service/\n        __init__.py\n        app.py\n    java_service/\n        __init__.py\n        app.java\n```\n\n#### Django Microservice\n\nCreate a Django project and app:\n\n```bash\ndjango-admin startproject django_app\npython manage.py startapp django_app\n```\n\nAdd `django_admin` to `INSTALLED_APPS` in `settings.py`:\n\n```python\nINSTALLED_APPS = [\n    # ...\n    'django_admin',\n]\n```\n\nCreate models, views, and admin interface:\n\n```python\n# models.py\nfrom django.db import models\n\nclass Product(models.Model):\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=255)\n    price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=5, decimal_places=2)\n\n# views.py\nfrom rest_framework.response import Response\nfrom rest_framework.views import APIView\nfrom .models import Product\n\nclass ProductList(APIView):\n    def get(self, request):\n        products = Product.objects.all()\n        return Response([{'id': p.id, 'name': p.name, 'price': p.price} for p in products])\n\n# admin.py\nfrom django.contrib import admin\nfrom .models import Product\n\nadmin.site.register(Product)\n```\n\nCreate admin interface:\n\n```python\n# urls.py\nfrom django.urls import path\nfrom django.contrib import admin\nfrom django.urls import include\n\nurlpatterns = [\n    path('admin/', admin.site.urls),\n    path('api/products/', include('django_app.urls')),\n]\n```\n\n#### GoLang Microservice\n\nCreate a new GoLang project and add a `main` function:\n\n```go\n// main.go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n    \"encoding/json\"\n    \"log\"\n    \"net/http\"\n\n    \"github.com/gorilla/mux\"\n)\n\ntype Product struct {\n    ID     int    `json:\"id\"`\n    Name   string `json:\"name\"`\n    Price  float64 `json:\"price\"`\n}\n\nvar products = []Product{\n    {ID: 1, Name: \"Product 1\", Price: 10.99},\n    {ID: 2, Name: \"Product 2\", Price: 9.99},\n}\n\nfunc main() {\n    router := mux.NewRouter()\n\n    router.HandleFunc(\"/products\", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {\n        json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(products)\n    })\n\n    log.Fatal(http.ListenAndServe(\":8080\", router))\n}\n```\n\n#### Java Spring Microservice\n\nCreate a new Java Spring project and add a `ProductController`:\n\n```java\n// ProductController.java\nimport org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;\nimport org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;\n\n@RestController\npublic class ProductController {\n    private static final String[] products = {\n        \"{\\\"id\\\":1,\\\"name\\\":\\\"Product 1\\\",\\\"price\\\":10.99}\",\n        \"{\\\"id\\\":2,\\\"name\\\":\\\"Product 2\\\",\\\"price\\\":9.99}\"\n    };\n\n    @GetMapping(\"/products\")\n    public String getProducts() {\n        return products[0];\n    }\n}\n```\n\n#### Connecting Microservices\n\nTo connect the microservices, you can use REST APIs or message queues like RabbitMQ. In this example, we'll use REST APIs.\n\nCreate a `Product` model in the Django app:\n\n```python\n# models.py\nfrom django.db import models\n\nclass Product(models.Model):\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=255)\n    price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=5, decimal_places=2)\n```\n\nCreate a `Product` model in the GoLang app:\n\n```go\n// main.go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n    \"encoding/json\"\n    \"log\"\n    \"net/http\"\n\n    \"github.com/gorilla/mux\"\n)\n\ntype Product struct {\n    ID     int    `json:\"id\"`\n    Name   string `json:\"name\"`\n    Price  float64 `json:\"price\"`\n}\n\nvar products = []Product{\n    {ID: 1, Name: \"Product 1\", Price: 10.99},\n    {ID: 2, Name: \"Product 2\", Price: 9.99},\n}\n\nfunc main() {\n    router := mux.NewRouter()\n\n    router.HandleFunc(\"/products\", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {\n        json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(products)\n    })\n\n    log.Fatal(http.ListenAndServe(\":8080\", router))\n}\n```\n\nCreate a `Product` model in the Java Spring app:\n\n```java\n// Product.java\npublic class Product {\n    private int id;\n    private String name;\n    private double price;\n\n    // Getters and setters\n}\n```\n\nCreate a REST API in the Django app to retrieve products:\n\n```python\n# views.py\nfrom rest_framework.response import Response\nfrom rest_framework.views import APIView\nfrom .models import Product\n\nclass ProductList(APIView):\n    def get(self, request):\n        products = Product.objects.all()\n        return Response([{'id': p.id, 'name': p.name, 'price': p.price} for p in products])\n```\n\nCreate a REST API in the GoLang app to retrieve products:\n\n```go\n// main.go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n    \"encoding/json\"\n    \"log\"\n    \"net/http\"\n\n    \"github.com/gorilla/mux\"\n)\n\ntype Product struct {\n    ID     int    `json:\"id\"`\n    Name   string `json:\"name\"`\n    Price  float64 `json:\"price\"`\n}\n\nvar products = []Product{\n    {ID: 1, Name: \"Product 1\", Price: 10.99},\n    {ID: 2, Name: \"Product 2\", Price: 9.99},\n}\n\nfunc main() {\n    router := mux.NewRouter()\n\n    router.HandleFunc(\"/products\", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {\n        json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(products)\n    })\n\n    log.Fatal(http.ListenAndServe(\":8080\", router))\n}\n```\n\nCreate a REST API in the Java Spring app to retrieve products:\n\n```java\n// ProductController.java\nimport org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;\nimport org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;\n\n@RestController\npublic class ProductController {\n    private static final String[] products = {\n        \"{\\\"id\\\":1,\\\"name\\\":\\\"Product 1\\\",\\\"price\\\":10.99}\",\n        \"{\\\"id\\\":2,\\\"name\\\":\\\"Product 2\\\",\\\"price\\\":9.99}\"\n    };\n\n    @GetMapping(\"/products\")\n    public String getProducts() {\n        return products[0];\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis is a basic example of a microservices architecture with Django, GoLang, and Java Spring. Each microservice has its own REST API to retrieve products. You can add more functionality to each microservice as needed."
