"```python\nfrom rest_framework.authtoken.models import Token\nfrom rest_framework.response import Response\nfrom rest_framework.views import APIView\nfrom rest_framework.permissions import IsAuthenticated\n\nclass RefreshTokenView(APIView):\n    permission_classes = [IsAuthenticated]\n\n    def post(self, request):\n        user = request.user\n        try:\n            token = Token.objects.get(user=user)\n            return Response({'refresh_token': token.key})\n        except Token.DoesNotExist:\n            return Response({'refresh_token': None}, status=404)\n```\n\nThis code creates a custom API view that listens for a POST request to the root URL. It checks if the user is authenticated and if a token exists for that user. If both conditions are met, it returns the refresh token in the response body. If not, it returns a 404 error with an empty refresh token in the response body."
"import subprocess\n\ndef run_command(command):\n    output = subprocess.check_output(command, shell=True)\n    return output.decode('utf-8')\n\n# Run the command to update the license file\nupdate_command = \"python -m setuptools --user --upgrade --upgrade-tools\"\noutput = run_command(update_command)\n\n# Run the command to install psycopg2 with the --plat-name flag\ninstall_command = \"python -m pip install --plat-name win-arm64 psycopg2\"\noutput = run_command(install_command)assistant\n\nThe issue here is that the `--plat-name` flag is not supported on Windows. The `--plat-name` flag is used to specify the platform name for building wheels, but it's not a valid flag for Windows.\n\nThe error message suggests that you should use the `win-amd64` platform instead, but this won't work for your project because it's a 32-bit Python interpreter.\n\nThe problem is that the `psycopg2` package is trying to build a wheel for the wrong platform. You can try to install the `psycopg2-binary` package instead, which is a pre-built wheel for Windows. \n\n```python\npip install psycopg2-binary\n```\n\nThis should install the `psycopg2` package without building a wheel from source."
