"```python\ndef main():\n    # Test case 1: StringBuilder with append method\n    a = \"Jav\"\n    a = \"\".join([\"J\", \"a\", \"v\"])  # equivalent to StringBuilder(\"Jav\").append(\"v\").toString()\n    a = a.intern()\n    c = \"Jav\"\n    print(a == c)\n\n    # Test case 2: StringBuilder with append method\n    a = \"Ja\"\n    a = a + \"v\"  # equivalent to StringBuilder(\"Ja\").append(\"v\").toString()\n    a = a.intern()\n    c = \"Jav\"\n    print(a == c)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nExplanation: The difference between the two test cases lies in how the string is created and interned.\n\nIn the first test case, a new string object is created using `StringBuilder(\"Jav\")`, and then the `append` method is called to add \"v\" to the string. The `toString()` method is then called to convert the `StringBuilder` object to a string. However, since `StringBuilder` creates a new string object each time it is used, the interned string reference is not created.\n\nIn the second test case, a new string object is created using `\"Ja\"`, and then the `+` operator is used to concatenate \"v\" to the string. This creates a new string object, but since the `+` operator creates a new string object, the interned string reference is not created either.\n\nHowever, when you use the `join()` method to concatenate the characters, it creates a new string object, but since the `join()` method takes an iterable of characters as an argument, it creates a new string object only once, and then reuses it. Therefore, the interned string reference is created.\n\nThis explains why the first test case prints `False` and the second test case prints `True`."
