"import timeit\n\ncode_1 = \"\"\"\ninitial_string = ''\nfor i in range(10000):\n    initial_string = initial_string + 'x' + 'y'\n\"\"\"\ncode_2 = \"\"\"\ninitial_string = ''\nfor i in range(10000):\n    initial_string += 'x' + 'y'\n\"\"\"\n\ntime_1 = timeit.timeit(code_1, number=100)\ntime_2 = timeit.timeit(code_2, number=100)\n\nprint(time_1)\nprint(time_2)assistant\n\nThe reason `+=` is more efficient than `=` in this case is due to the way Python handles string concatenation. When you use `+=`, Python knows that you're working with a string and can take advantage of the fact that strings are immutable. This means that Python can reuse the existing string object and only modify it in-place, rather than creating a new string object every time.\n\nOn the other hand, when you use `=`, Python has to create a new string object every time, which can be slower.\n\nIn the case of the first example, both `+=` and `=` are used for string concatenation, so the performance difference is likely due to other factors such as the specific Python implementation and the hardware.\n\nHowever, when you use `+=` for the second example, where you're only concatenating `'x'` to the string, the performance difference is more pronounced. This is because the `+=` operation is able to take advantage of the fact that the string is already mutable, allowing it to modify the existing string object in-place.\n\nThe behavior you're seeing with different Python versions is likely due to changes in the way Python handles string concatenation in different versions. For example, in Python 3.7-3.9, the `+=` operation for strings uses a more efficient algorithm that takes advantage of the fact that strings are immutable, but this algorithm is not available in earlier versions of Python. In Python 3.10 and later, the `+=` operation for strings uses a different algorithm that is slower than the one used in earlier versions."
