"def is_palindromic(arr):\n    for i in range(len(arr) - 1):\n        if len(arr[i]) > 1 and len(arr[i + 1]) > 1:\n            if arr[i][-1] != arr[i + 1][0]:\n                return False\n            if len(arr[i]) > 2 and len(arr[i + 1]) > 2:\n                if arr[i][-2] == arr[i + 1][1]:\n                    return False\n    return True\n\n# test the function\narr = [\"aa\", \"bab\", \"cde\", \"aba\", \"ab\"]\nprint(is_palindromic(arr))assistant\n\nThis function checks each pair of consecutive elements in the array. If the last character of the first string is not equal to the first character of the second string, it immediately returns False. If the lengths of the strings are both greater than 1 and the second character of the first string is equal to the second character of the second string, it also returns False. If none of these conditions are met, it returns True, indicating that the array can be made palindromic by performing the given operations."
"```python\nimport os\n\ndef add_leading_zeros(file_path):\n    with open(file_path, 'r') as file:\n        lines = file.readlines()\n\n    modified_lines = []\n    for i, line in enumerate(lines, start=1):\n        modified_line = f\"{i:05d}:{line.strip()}\"\n        modified_lines.append(modified_line)\n\n    with open(file_path, 'w') as file:\n        file.writelines(modified_lines)\n\n# Usage\nadd_leading_zeros('file.txt')\n```"
