"```python\nimport threading\n\nclass PetersonLock:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.left = False\n        self.right = False\n\n    def lock(self):\n        while self.left and self.right:\n            if self.left:\n                self.left, self.right = self.right, self.left\n                self.left = False\n            else:\n                # acquire\n                pass\n\n    def unlock(self):\n        if not self.right:\n            self.right = True\n\ndef increment(counter, lock):\n    for _ in range(1000):\n        lock.lock()\n        try:\n            counter[0] += 1\n        finally:\n            lock.unlock()\n\ndef main():\n    counter = [0]\n    lock = PetersonLock()\n\n    t0 = threading.Thread(target=increment, args=(counter, lock))\n    t1 = threading.Thread(target=increment, args=(counter, lock))\n\n    t0.start()\n    t1.start()\n\n    t0.join()\n    t1.join()\n\n    print(\"Counter:\", counter[0])\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nPeterson's algorithm is a synchronization mechanism that provides mutual exclusion among processes/threads. While it can be used to protect critical sections, it's not commonly used in production code due to its potential for deadlock and livelock. The provided Raku example demonstrates how Peterson's algorithm can be used to protect a critical section, but it's not recommended for use in real-world applications."
