"```python\nimport threading\n\nclass BaseClass:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._stop = threading.Event()\n        self._thread = threading.Thread(target=self._worker)\n\n    def _worker(self):\n        while not self._stop.is_set():\n            # Just to help reproduce the race condition.\n            import time\n            time.sleep(0.05)\n            self.step()\n\n    def start(self):\n        self._thread.start()\n\n    def stop(self):\n        self._stop.set()\n\n    def step(self):\n        # This method must be implemented by derived classes\n        pass\n\nclass DerivedClass(BaseClass):\n    def step(self):\n        print(\"Step!\")\n\ndef main():\n    base = DerivedClass()\n    base.start()\n\n    # Just to help reproduce the race condition.\n    import time\n    time.sleep(1.1)\n\n    base.stop()\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nThis code achieves the same result as your C++ code. The `BaseClass` class manages the thread and stops it when the `stop` method is called. The `step` method is declared as an abstract method, which must be implemented by derived classes. In this example, we use the `DerivedClass` to demonstrate how to use the `BaseClass`. The `stop` method is called after a short delay to simulate the race condition."
