"```python\nimport threading\n\nclass Foo:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n\nclass Condition:\n    def __init__(self, lock):\n        self.lock = lock\n        self.waiting = []\n\n    def check(self, foo):\n        with self.lock:\n            return foo.value > 0\n\n    def wait(self, foo):\n        with self.lock:\n            self.waiting.append(foo)\n            foo.waiting = True\n\nclass ListWrapper:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.list = []\n        self.lock = threading.Lock()\n        self.condition = Condition(self.lock)\n\n    def get_foo(self, condition):\n        while True:\n            with self.lock:\n                if self.list and condition.check(self.list[-1]):\n                    return self.list.pop()\n            if not self.condition.waiting:\n                break\n\n    def refresh(self, fetch_from_db):\n        with self.lock:\n            self.list = fetch_from_db\n```\n\nExplanation: \nThis code implements a thread-safe list using a custom `ListWrapper` class. The list is protected by a lock, and a condition variable is used to wait for the list to be updated. The `get_foo` method waits on the condition variable until an element meets the condition, and the `refresh` method updates the list. This approach avoids the need for synchronized methods and provides thread safety without the overhead of `CopyOnWriteArrayList`. \n\nNote that the `Foo` class is simplified to just store a value, but it could be any object. The `Condition` class is used to check if an element meets a condition, and it also handles waiting on the condition variable. The `ListWrapper` class manages the list, lock, and condition variable."
