"import numpy as np\n\ndef calculate_centroid(points):\n    # Calculate the area of the polygon using the Shoelace formula\n    n = len(points)\n    area = 0.0\n    for i in range(n):\n        j = (i + 1) % n\n        area += points[i][0] * points[j][1]\n        area -= points[j][0] * points[i][1]\n    area = abs(area) / 2.0\n\n    # Calculate the centroid\n    centroid_x = np.sum([points[i][0] for i in range(n)]) / (2.0 * area)\n    centroid_y = np.sum([points[i][1] for i in range(n)]) / (2.0 * area)\n\n    return centroid_x, centroid_y\n\npoints = np.array([(0,0), (70,0), (70,25), (45,45), (45,180), (95,188), (95,200), (-25,200), (-25, 188), (25,180), (25,45), (0,25), (0,0)])\ncentroid_x, centroid_y = calculate_centroid(points)\nprint(\"Centroid:\", (centroid_x, centroid_y))assistant\n\nThis code calculates the centroid of the polygon by first calculating its area using the Shoelace formula. It then uses this area to calculate the x and y coordinates of the centroid. The result should be more accurate than the previous code."
