"import numpy as np\n\nx = np.arange(720).reshape((2,3,4,5,6))\n\nprint(x[:, :, :, 0, [0, 1, 2, 4, 5]].shape)  # Output: (2, 3, 4, 5)\nprint(x[:, :, 0, :, [0, 1, 2, 4, 5]].shape)  # Output: (5, 2, 3, 5)assistant\n\nThe difference in the shapes of the output is due to the way NumPy handles broadcasting when using mixed indexing.\n\nIn the first case, `x[:, :, :, 0, [0, 1, 2, 4, 5]]`, the indexing operation is performed from left to right. The `:` index is broadcasted to the shape of the array to select all elements in the first dimension. The `0` index is then used to select the first slice in the fourth dimension. The `[0, 1, 2, 4, 5]` index is also broadcasted to the shape of the array to select all elements in the fifth dimension. This results in a shape of `(2, 3, 4, 5)`.\n\nIn the second case, `x[:, :, 0, :, [0, 1, 2, 4, 5]]`, the indexing operation is also performed from left to right. However, the `:` index is not broadcasted to the shape of the array to select all elements in the first dimension. Instead, it is used as an index to select all slices in the first dimension. The `0` index is then used to select the first slice in the third dimension. The `[0, 1, 2, 4, 5]` index is broadcasted to the shape of the array to select all elements in the fifth dimension. This results in a shape of `(5, 2, 3, 5)`.\n\nThere is no explicit documentation for the broadcasting rules in NumPy's fancy indexing, but it can be understood by analyzing the behavior of the indexing operations and the shapes of the resulting arrays."
"```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport numba as nb\nimport benchit\nnb.set_num_threads(1)\n\n@nb.njit\ndef numba_operation(in_arr, out):\n    for i in range(out.shape[0]):\n        for j in range(out.shape[1]):\n            out[i,j] += in_arr[i,j] * 2 + 4\n            \n@nb.njit\ndef numba_operation_with_zeros(in_arr, out):\n    for i in range(out.shape[0]):\n        for j in range(out.shape[1]):\n            out[i,j] = 0\n    for i in range(out.shape[0]):\n        for j in range(out.shape[1]):\n            out[i,j] += in_arr[i,j] * 2 + 4\n\ndef every_time_generate_zeros(data):\n    in_arr, out = data\n    out = np.zeros(shape=(out.shape[0], out.shape[0]))\n    numba_operation(in_arr, out)\n    return out\n\ndef make_zeros_numba(data):\n    in_arr, out = data\n    numba_operation_with_zeros(in_arr, out)\n\ndef generate_arrays(n):\n    in_arr = np.random.rand(2**n, 2**n)\n    out = np.random.rand(2**n, 2**n)\n    return in_arr, out\n\nt = benchit.timings([every_time_generate_zeros, make_zeros_numba], \n                    {n:generate_arrays(n) for n in np.arange(9, 15, 1)}, \n                    input_name='2^n')\nt.plot(modules=benchit.extract_modules_from_globals(globals()))\n```\n\nThis performance difference is due to the fact that `np.zeros()` uses the `calloc` function under the hood, which is a low-level memory allocation function that can be optimized by the compiler. On the other hand, re-initializing an existing array by setting all elements to zero is a more complex operation that involves multiple steps, including memory allocation and copying data. \n\nThe acceleration comes from the fact that `calloc` is optimized for arrays and can be done in a single operation, whereas re-initializing an array involves multiple steps that are not as optimized. This is why `np.zeros()` is generally faster than re-initializing an existing array."
