"```python\ndef vapply_sapply_circumstances():\n    # vapply can be slower than sapply in the following circumstances:\n    # 1. When the input vectors are large and the type of the result is not specified,\n    #    because R has to check the types of the elements in the input vectors,\n    #    while sapply only checks the type of the result.\n    # 2. When the function being applied is not vectorized,\n    #    because vapply has to use loops to apply the function element-wise,\n    #    while sapply uses vectorized operations.\n    # 3. When memory allocation is expensive,\n    #    because vapply has to allocate memory for the result,\n    #    while sapply does not.\n\n    # Example code to demonstrate the difference:\n    import numpy as np\n    import time\n\n    # Define a function that is not vectorized\n    def not_vectorized(x, y):\n        return x + y\n\n    # Generate large input vectors\n    x = np.random.rand(1000000)\n    y = np.random.rand(1000000)\n\n    # Measure the time taken by vapply\n    start_time = time.time()\n    vapply(x, not_vectorized, np.int64)\n    end_time = time.time()\n    print(f\"Time taken by vapply: {end_time - start_time} seconds\")\n\n    # Measure the time taken by sapply\n    start_time = time.time()\n    sapply(x, not_vectorized, lambda x: x + y)\n    end_time = time.time()\n    print(f\"Time taken by sapply: {end_time - start_time} seconds\")\n```"
