"def s_gen(dataset, Signal):\n    _status = 0\n    status0 = []\n    for (i) in Signal:\n        if _status == 0:\n            if i == 1:\n                _status = 1 \n        elif _status == 1:\n            if (i == 2 or i==3):\n                _status = 0\n        status0.append(_status)\n        \n    dataset['status0'] = status0\n\n    return dataset['status0']\n\ndf['Status'] = df.groupby('Machine')['Signal'].apply(s_gen)\ndf = df.drop('status0',axis=1)assistant\n\nThis code uses the `groupby` function to group the dataframe by the 'Machine' column, then applies the `s_gen` function to each group. The result is a new 'Status' column for each machine."
"```python\ndef delete_first_column(df_list):\n    for df in df_list:\n        df = df.iloc[:, 1:]\n\ndef main():\n    list_of_dfs = ls()[sapply(ls(), function(x) class(get(x)) == 'data.frame')]\n    delete_first_column(list_of_dfs)\n\nmain()\n```\n\nNote: The `ls()` function is not a standard Python function, it seems to be a function from the `stats` package in R. If you are using Python, you should replace it with the actual function or code that generates the list of dataframes. \n\nAlso, this code will create a new dataframe without the first column, but it won't modify the original dataframe. If you want to modify the original dataframe, you can use the following code instead:\n\n```python\ndef delete_first_column(df_list):\n    for i, df in enumerate(df_list):\n        df_list[i] = df.iloc[:, 1:]\n\ndef main():\n    list_of_dfs = ls()[sapply(ls(), function(x) class(get(x)) == 'data.frame')]\n    delete_first_column(list_of_dfs)\n```"
