"import urllib.parse\nfrom urllib.parse import urlparse, urlunparse\n\ndef fix_url(url):\n    parsed_url = urlparse(url)\n    path = parsed_url.path\n    query = urllib.parse.parse_qs(parsed_url.query)\n    query['search_term'] = [query['search_term'][0]]\n    path = path.replace('%20', '+')\n    path = path.replace('+', '%20')\n    new_query = urllib.parse.urlencode(query, doseq=True)\n    new_path = urllib.parse.quote(path)\n    new_url = urlunparse((parsed_url.scheme, parsed_url.netloc, new_path, parsed_url.params, new_query, parsed_url.fragment))\n    return new_url\n\n# Test the function\nurl = \"http://closefocusresearch.com/search/node/body%20armor\"\nprint(fix_url(url))assistant\n\nThis code replaces the `%20` in the URL with `+` (which is the standard URL encoding for spaces) and then back-replaces it with `%20` to match the original URL format. It then uses `urlunparse` to reconstruct the URL with the modified path and query string.\n\nPlease note that this solution assumes that the search term is passed as a query parameter named `search_term`. If the search term is passed differently, you may need to adjust the code accordingly."
