"```python\nimport subprocess\n\ndef convert_columns(input_file, columns, separator=';'):\n    # Use AWK to split the input file into multiple columns\n    command = f'awk -v cols={columns} -v sep={separator} \\'NR%cols {{printf $0\\\"\\\",\\\";next}} 1\\' {input_file}'\n    subprocess.run(command, shell=True)\n```"
"```python\nimport csv\n\n# Specify the paths to the old and new CSV files\nold_csv_file_path = 'old_data.csv'\nnew_csv_file_path = 'new_data.csv'\n\n# Create a list of required headers\nrequired_headers = ['Column B', 'Column D']\n\n# Create a writer for the new CSV file\nwith open(new_csv_file_path, 'w', newline='') as new_file:\n    writer = csv.writer(new_file)\n    \n    # Read the records from the old file\n    with open(old_csv_file_path, 'r') as old_file:\n        reader = csv.DictReader(old_file)\n        \n        # Write the selected data to the new file\n        writer.writerow(required_headers)  # Write the header row\n        for row in reader:\n            if all(row.get(header) is not None for header in required_headers):\n                writer.writerow({header: row[header] for header in required_headers})\n```\n\nThis code reads the old CSV file, filters out the rows that do not match the required headers, and writes the remaining rows to the new CSV file. The `DictReader` class is used to easily access the values in each row by their header names."
"```bash\nawk -F \",\" '\n    FNR==NR {\n        a[$1]=$2\n        next\n    }\n    { \n        print $0\",\"(($1 in a)?a[$1]:\"NA\")\n    }\n' File1.csv File2.csv > New.csv\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n1. We start with `awk`, which is the command-line utility for processing and transforming text data.\n2. `-F \",\"` tells `awk` to use the comma as the field separator. This means it will split each line into fields based on the commas.\n3. `FNR==NR` checks if this is the first record (line) in the first file (`File1.csv`). If it is, then we set the value of `a[$1]` to `$2`, which is the second field of the line. The `next` command tells `awk` to move on to the next line without processing further.\n4. The second block of code (`{ ... }`) is executed for every line in both files.\n5. `print $0` prints the entire line.\n6. `(($1 in a)?a[$1]:\"NA\")` is a ternary expression that checks if the first field (`$1`) exists in the array `a`. If it does, then it prints the corresponding value from `a` (`a[$1]`). If not, it prints \"NA\" (Not Available). \n\nNote that the ternary expression works as follows:\n\n- `($1 in a)` checks if the key `$1` exists in the array `a`. If it does, then the expression evaluates to `1` (true), otherwise it evaluates to `0` (false).\n- The expression then uses this result to decide which value to print: if it's `1`, then it prints `a[$1]`, if it's `0`, then it prints `\"NA\"`."
"```python\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\ndef clean_csv(file_path):\n    try:\n        # Read the CSV file into a pandas DataFrame\n        df = pd.read_csv(file_path)\n\n        # Replace invalid date formats with NaN\n        df['date'] = pd.to_datetime(df['date'], errors='coerce')\n\n        # Remove rows with missing dates\n        df = df.dropna(subset=['date'])\n\n        # Convert date column to string format\n        df['date'] = df['date'].dt.strftime('%Y-%m-%d')\n\n        return df.to_csv(index=False)\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"An error occurred: {e}\")\n        return None\n\n# Usage\ncleaned_data = clean_csv('data.csv')\nprint(cleaned_data)\n```"
