"```python\nTRIAL = [[{'http': '46.101.160.223:80', 'https': '46.101.160.223:80'}, 0],\n         [{'http': '66.70.178.214:9300', 'https': '66.70.178.214:9300'}, 0],\n         [{'http': '130.61.100.135:80', 'https': '130.61.100.135:80'}, 0],\n         [{'http': '157.245.27.9:3128', 'https': '157.245.27.9:3128'}, 0],\n         [{'http': '185.246.84.7:8080', 'https': '185.246.84.7:8080'}, 0],\n         [{'http': '185.246.84.7:8080', 'https': '185.246.84.7:8080'}, 0],\n         [{'http': '130.61.100.135:80', 'https': '130.61.100.135:80'}, 1]]\n\nTRIAL = [i for i in TRIAL if i[0] not in [item[0] for item in TRIAL]]\n```\n\nThis one-liner uses list comprehension to filter out duplicate lists from the original list. The condition `i[0] not in [item[0] for item in TRIAL]` checks if the dictionary in each sublist is already present in the list of sublists. If it's not present, the sublist is included in the new list."
"```python\nimport pandas as pd\nimport re\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'Original': [92,93,94,95,100,101,102],\n             'Sub_90': [99,98,99,100,102,101,np.nan],\n             'Sub_80': [99,98,99,100,102,np.nan,np.nan],\n             'Gen_90': [99,98,99,100,102,101,101],\n             'Gen_80': [99,98,99,100,102,101,100]})\n\n# Create dictionary with pairs from column from pandas dataframe using regex\npairs = {}\nfor col in ['Sub_70', 'Sub_60', 'Sub_50', 'Sub_40', 'Sub_30', 'Sub_20', 'Sub_10',\n            'Gen_70', 'Gen_60', 'Gen_50', 'Gen_40', 'Gen_30', 'Gen_20', 'Gen_10']:\n    pattern = r'Gen_(\\d+)'  # Pattern to match Gen columns\n    matches = df[col].apply(lambda x: re.search(pattern, str(x)).group(1))  # Extract the number from each Gen column\n    pairs[col] = {'Original': 'Original', 'Gen': f'Gen_{matches}'}\n\n# Print the dictionary\nfor key, value in pairs.items():\n    print(f'{key}: {value}')\n```"
"```python\n# This is not possible in Python as it does not support map literals with functions\n# However, we can achieve similar behavior using dictionaries and functions\n\ndef get_key():\n    return \"key\"\n\ndef main():\n    blaa = {\n        \"key\": \"value\",\n        get_key(): \"value2\"  # This will raise a KeyError at runtime\n    }\n    print(\"Hello, \" + blaa[\"key\"])\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nIn Python, map literals are not directly supported. However, we can achieve similar behavior using dictionaries and functions. When you try to use a function as a key in a dictionary, Python will raise a `KeyError` at runtime."
