"```javascript\nimport { Test, TestingModule } from '@nestjs/testing';\nimport { AppModule } from './app.module';\n\ndescribe('AppController', () => {\n  let app: TestingModule;\n\n  beforeEach(async () => {\n    app = await Test.createTestingModule({\n      imports: [AppModule],\n    }).compile();\n  });\n\n  it('should be defined', () => {\n    expect(app.get(AppController)).toBeDefined();\n  });\n});\n```\n\nExplanation:\nThis is a basic unit test for a NestJS application. It imports the necessary modules from `@nestjs/testing` and creates a test module with the `AppModule`. The `beforeEach` hook is used to compile the test module before each test. The test itself checks if the `AppController` is defined in the test module."
"import unittest\nfrom unittest import TestCase\nfrom common_letters import three_most_common, NotEnoughLetters\n\nclass TestCommonLetters(TestCase):\n\n    # valid input\n    def test_good_string(self):\n        expected_input = \"cheesy puff\"\n        expected_output = \"Here are your most common letters: 1) e  2) f  3) c\"\n        result = three_most_common(expected_input)\n        self.assertEqual(expected_output, result)\n\n    # invalid input\n    def test_bad_string(self):\n        expected_input = \"cheesy puff\"\n        false_output = \"Here are your most common letters: 1) f  2) f  3) e\"\n        result = three_most_common(expected_input)\n        self.assertNotEqual(false_output, result)\n\n    # edge case 1, having 3 letters\n    def test_having_three_letters(self):\n        expected_input = \"hay\"\n        expected_output = \"Here are your most common letters: 1) h  2) a  3) y\"\n        result = three_most_common(expected_input)\n        self.assertEqual(expected_output, result)\n\n    # edge case 2, having 2 letters\n    def test_having_two_letters(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(NotEnoughLetters):\n            three_most_common(string=\"ha\")assistant\n\nThe issue lies in how you're calling the `three_most_common` function in your test case. You're passing the string as an argument, but in the function definition, you're expecting the string to be a parameter of the function.\n\nIn Python, when you define a function, the parameters are listed after the function name, but when you call the function, you pass the arguments before the function name. So, in your test case, you're passing `string=\"ha\"` as an argument to `three_most_common`, but the function definition is expecting the string to be passed as an argument to the `three_most_common` function.\n\nTo fix this, you should remove the `string=` from the call to `three_most_common` in your test case. Here's the corrected line:\n\n```python\nwith self.assertRaises(NotEnoughLetters):\n    three_most_common(\"ha\")\n```"
"```python\nimport unittest\n\nclass A:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n\nclass A_bundle:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.objects = []\n\n    def add_object(self, obj):\n        self.objects.append(obj)\n\ndef create_test_A():\n    return A(1)\n\nclass TestA(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test(self):\n        new_A = create_test_A()\n        self.assertEqual(new_A.value, 1)\n\nclass TestA_bundle(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_add_object(self):\n        new_A1 = create_test_A()\n        new_A2 = create_test_A()\n        bundle = A_bundle()\n        bundle.add_object(new_A1)\n        bundle.add_object(new_A2)\n        self.assertEqual(len(bundle.objects), 2)\n        self.assertIn(new_A1, bundle.objects)\n        self.assertIn(new_A2, bundle.objects)\n\n    def test_add_object_twice(self):\n        new_A1 = create_test_A()\n        new_A2 = create_test_A()\n        bundle = A_bundle()\n        bundle.add_object(new_A1)\n        bundle.add_object(new_A2)\n        bundle.add_object(new_A1)  # This should not affect the first object\n        self.assertEqual(len(bundle.objects), 2)\n        self.assertIn(new_A1, bundle.objects)\n        self.assertIn(new_A2, bundle.objects)\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    unittest.main()\n```"
"```python\nimport re\n\ndef match_log_values(expected_log, actual_log):\n    expected_pattern = re.compile(rf\"Encountered {expected_log['exceptionType']}. Unable to verify user with id {expected_log['userId']}\")\n    actual_pattern = re.compile(rf\"Encountered {actual_log['exceptionType']}. Unable to verify user with id {actual_log['userId']}\")\n\n    return expected_pattern.match(actual_log['message']) is not None and actual_pattern.match(actual_log['message']) is not None\n\n# Example usage:\nexpected_log = {\n    'exceptionType': 'NullReferenceException',\n    'userId': 1\n}\n\nactual_log = {\n    'message': \"Encountered NullReferenceException. Unable to verify user with id 1\"\n}\n\nassert match_log_values(expected_log, actual_log)\n```"
