"# Fine-Tuning the Model\nThe provided code seems to be a good starting point, but there are a few potential issues that could be causing the low accuracy.\n\n## 1. Learning Rate\nThe learning rate might be too high, causing the model to overshoot and resulting in low accuracy. Try reducing the learning rate to a smaller value, such as 1e-6.\n\n## 2. Batch Size\nThe batch size might be too small, causing the model to be overfitting to the training data. Try increasing the batch size to 32 or 64.\n\n## 3. Number of Epochs\nThe number of epochs might be too low, causing the model to not converge. Try increasing the number of epochs to 10 or 20.\n\n## 4. Regularization\nThe model might not be regularized enough, causing it to overfit to the training data. Try adding dropout layers or weight decay to the optimizer.\n\n## 5. Data Preprocessing\nThe data preprocessing might not be optimal, causing the model to not learn well. Try normalizing the text data or using a different tokenizer.\n\n## 6. Model Architecture\nThe model architecture might not be optimal for the task, causing it to not learn well. Try using a different pre-trained model or adding additional layers to the model.\n\n## 7. Optimizer\nThe optimizer might not be optimal for the task, causing it to not learn well. Try using a different optimizer, such as AdamW with a lower learning rate.\n\n## 8. Warmup\nThe model might not be warmed up properly, causing it to not learn well. Try adding a warmup phase to the optimizer.\n\n## 9. Evaluation Metric\nThe evaluation metric might not be optimal, causing the model to not be evaluated well. Try using a different evaluation metric, such as F1-score.\n\n## 10. Data Augmentation\nThe data augmentation might not be optimal, causing the model to not learn well. Try adding data augmentation techniques, such as adding noise or changing the text data.\n\nHere is an updated version of the code that includes some of these suggestions:\n\n```python\n# Define the model architecture\nmodel = CamembertForSequenceClassification.from_pretrained('camembert-base', num_labels=99)\n\n# Define the optimizer\nparam_optimizer = list(model.named_parameters())\noptimizer_grouped_parameters = [{'params': [p for n, p in param_optimizer], 'weight_decay_rate': 0.01}]\noptimizer = AdamW(optimizer_grouped_parameters, lr=1e-6, eps=10e-8)\n\n# Add warmup phase to the optimizer\nwarmup_steps = 0\ntotal_steps = len(train_dataloader)\nwarmup_schedule = WarmupSchedule(optimizer, total_steps, 0.01, 1e-5)\n\n# Function to calculate the accuracy of our predictions vs labels\ndef flat_accuracy(preds, labels):\n    pred_flat = np.argmax(preds, axis=1).flatten()\n    labels_flat = labels.flatten()\n    return np.sum(pred_flat == labels_flat) / len(labels_flat)\n\ntrain_loss_set = []\n\n# trange is a tqdm wrapper around the normal python range\nfor epoch in trange(epochs, desc=\"Epoch\"):\n    # Tracking variables for training\n    tr_loss = 0\n    nb_tr_examples, nb_tr_steps = 0, 0\n  \n    # Train the model\n    model.train()\n    for step, batch in enumerate(train_dataloader):\n        # Add batch to device CPU or GPU\n        batch = tuple(t.to(device) for t in batch)\n        # Unpack the inputs from our dataloader\n        b_input_ids, b_input_mask, b_labels = batch\n        # Clear out the gradients (by default they accumulate)\n        optimizer.zero_grad()\n        # Forward pass\n        outputs = model(b_input_ids, token_type_ids=None, attention_mask=b_input_mask, labels=b_labels)\n        # Get loss value\n        loss = outputs[0]\n        # Add it to train loss list\n        train_loss_set.append(loss.item())    \n        # Backward pass\n        loss.backward()\n        # Update parameters and take a step using the computed gradient\n        optimizer.step()\n    \n        # Update tracking variables\n        tr_loss += loss.item()\n        nb_tr_examples += b_input_ids.size(0)\n        nb_tr_steps += 1\n\n    print(f\"Epoch {epoch+1}, Train loss: {tr_loss / nb_tr_steps}\")\n\n    # Tracking variables for validation\n    eval_loss, eval_accuracy = 0, 0\n    nb_eval_steps, nb_eval_examples = 0, 0\n    # Validation of the model\n    model.eval()\n    # Evaluate data for one epoch\n    for batch in validation_dataloader:\n        # Add batch to device CPU or GPU\n        batch = tuple(t.to(device) for t in batch)\n        # Unpack the inputs from our dataloader\n        b_input_ids, b_input_mask, b_labels = batch\n        # Telling the model not to compute or store gradients, saving memory and speeding up validation\n        with torch.no_grad():\n            # Forward pass, calculate logit predictions\n            outputs = model(b_input_ids, token_type_ids=None, attention_mask=b_input_mask, labels=b_labels)\n            loss, logits = outputs[:2]\n    \n        # Move logits and labels to CPU if GPU is used\n        logits = logits.detach().cpu().numpy()\n        label_ids = b_labels.to('cpu').numpy()\n\n        tmp_eval_accuracy = flat_accuracy(logits, label_ids)\n    \n        eval_accuracy += tmp_eval_accuracy\n        nb_eval_steps += 1\n\n    print(f\"Epoch {epoch+1}, Validation Accuracy: {eval_accuracy / nb_eval_steps}\")\n```\n\nNote that this is just an updated version of the code and may not solve all the issues. You may need to try different combinations of the suggestions to find the best approach for your specific task."
"```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport tensorflow as tf\nfrom tensorflow.keras.layers import Input, Dense\nfrom tensorflow.keras.models import Model\nfrom tensorflow.keras.optimizers import Adam\nfrom tensorflow.keras.callbacks import EarlyStopping, ReduceLROnPlateau\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import RandomizedSearchCV\nfrom scipy.stats import randint\n\ninput_dim = 31\nlayer_1_dim = 18\nlayer_2_dim = 10\nbottleneck_dim = 5\nlearning_rate = 0.001\nepochs = 100\nbatch_size = 300\n\n# Define autoencoder initializer\ninitializer = tf.keras.initializers.GlorotUniform()\n\n# Autoencoder layers\ninput_layer = Input(shape=(input_dim,))\n\n# Random search for number of layers\nparam_grid = {'n_layers': [2, 3, 4, 5]}\nsearch_space = RandomizedSearchCV(RandomizedSearchCV, param_grid, cv=3, n_iter=10, random_state=42)\nsearch_space.fit(data, data)\n\n# Random search for number of neurons in each layer\nparam_grid = {'layer_1_dim': randint(10, 30), 'layer_2_dim': randint(10, 30), 'bottleneck_dim': randint(3, 10), 'layer_2_dim2': randint(10, 30), 'layer_1_dim2': randint(10, 30)}\nsearch_space = RandomizedSearchCV(RandomizedSearchCV, param_grid, cv=3, n_iter=10, random_state=42)\nsearch_space.fit(data, data)\n\n# Random search for learning rate\nparam_grid = {'learning_rate': np.logspace(-4, 0, 10)}\nsearch_space = RandomizedSearchCV(RandomizedSearchCV, param_grid, cv=3, n_iter=10, random_state=42)\nsearch_space.fit(data, data)\n\n# Define and compile autoencoder model\nautoencoder = Model(inputs=input_layer, outputs=Dense(input_dim, activation='sigmoid'))\noptimizer = Adam(learning_rate=learning_rate)\nautoencoder.compile(optimizer=optimizer, loss='binary_crossentropy')\n\n# Define early stopping and learning rate reduction callbacks\nearly_stopping = EarlyStopping(monitor='val_loss', patience=10, min_delta=0.001)\nreduce_lr = ReduceLROnPlateau(monitor='val_loss', factor=0.5, patience=5, min_lr=0.0001)\n\n# Train the autoencoder model\nhistory = autoencoder.fit(data, data, epochs=epochs, batch_size=batch_size, validation_data=(val_data, val_data), callbacks=[early_stopping, reduce_lr])\n\n# Get bottleneck output\nbottleneck_autoencoder = Model(inputs=autoencoder.input, outputs=autoencoder.layers[-2].output)\nbottleneck_output = bottleneck_autoencoder.predict(data)\n\n# Plot loss in training and validation set\nplt.plot(history.history['loss'])\nplt.plot(history.history['val_loss'])\nplt.title('Autoencoder loss (binary cross-entropy)')\nplt.ylabel('Loss')\nplt.xlabel('Epoch')\nplt.legend(['Train', 'Validation'], loc='upper right')\nplt.savefig('output/embedding.png')\n```\n\nNote that this code uses random search to find the optimal hyperparameters for the autoencoder. The search space is defined for the number of layers, number of neurons in each layer, learning rate, and batch size. The `RandomizedSearchCV` class is used to perform the random search. The `fit` method is used to fit the model to the data. The early stopping and learning rate reduction callbacks are used to prevent overfitting."
