"```python\nimport pickle\nimport struct\n\ndef read_corrupted_pickle(file_path, max_lines):\n    with open(file_path, 'rb') as f:\n        while True:\n            try:\n                line = pickle.load(f)\n                yield line\n            except pickle.UnpicklingError as e:\n                if e.args[0] == b'\\x00':\n                    # Skip this line\n                    pass\n                else:\n                    raise\n            except struct.error:\n                # Skip this line\n                pass\n            if len(line) != 4:  # Assuming each tuple has a score, sentence, username, and datetime\n                break\n\npath = \"/path/to/file.pkl\"\nmax_lines = 1000  # Maximum number of lines to read\nfor line in read_corrupted_pickle(path, max_lines):\n    print(line)\n```\n\nThis script reads the pickle file line by line and skips any lines that cannot be unpickled due to a null value. It assumes that each tuple in the set contains a score, sentence, username, and datetime, which are separated by a null byte. \n\nPlease note that manually editing the file might be a more reliable method, especially if you are not familiar with the pickle format. You can use a hex editor to manually remove the null bytes from the corrupted lines."
