"```python\nclass Token:\n    def __init__(self, symbol, convert):\n        self.symbol = symbol\n        self.convert = convert\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return f\"Token({self.symbol}, {self.convert})\"\n\ndef main():\n    egal_token = Token(\"One\", 111)\n    print(eagal_token)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nThe issue arises because both `symbol` and `convert` are instance methods in the `Token` class. In your code, you are trying to use both `symbol` and `convert` in the same instance. However, you are also trying to use both methods in the `main` function. This is causing the ambiguity error because Haskell does not know which method to use when you use both names.\n\nA possible solution is to create two distinct classes, one for the token data and one for the token functionality. This way, you can avoid the ambiguity error.\n\n```haskell\ndata Egal = One | Two\n\nclass TokenData a where\n    symbol :: a -> String\n\nclass Token a where\n    convert :: a -> b\n    instanceToken :: TokenData a => Token a b\n\ninstance TokenData Egal where\n    symbol One = \"one\"\n    symbol Two = \"two\"\n\ninstance Token Egal Int where\n    convert One = 111\n    convert Two = 222\n\ninstance Token Egal Int where\n    instanceToken = Token convert\n```\n\nHowever, a more common solution is to use a single class with a type parameter and pattern matching for the instance methods.\n\n```haskell\nclass Token a where\n    convert :: a -> b\n    symbol :: a -> String\n\ninstance Token Egal Int where\n    convert One = 111\n    convert Two = 222\n    symbol One = \"one\"\n    symbol Two = \"two\"\n```"
