"```javascript\nclass Secure {\n    #privateProperty = 4;\n\n    #privateMethod() {\n        console.log('The property ' + this.#privateProperty + ' should not be accessible outside this class');\n    }\n}\n\nconst secure = new Secure();\nsecure.#privateMethod();\n```\n\nThe reason for this behavior lies in how JavaScript handles private fields and methods. In JavaScript, private fields and methods are not enforced by the language itself. Instead, they rely on the surrounding class to maintain their encapsulation.\n\nWhen you try to call `secure.#privateMethod()` immediately after creating the `secure` object, it throws an error because the `#privateMethod` is not yet defined when it's called. This is because the JavaScript engine only checks for the existence of a property or method at runtime, not at compile time.\n\nHowever, when you access `#privateMethod` through the console, it's treated as a property, not a method. This is because the JavaScript engine can access the property at runtime, allowing it to evaluate the expression `The property 4 should not be accessible outside this class` correctly.\n\nTo achieve similar behavior in a more controlled way, you can use a wrapper function to call the private method:\n\n```javascript\nclass Secure {\n    #privateProperty = 4;\n\n    #privateMethod() {\n        console.log('The property ' + this.#privateProperty + ' should not be accessible outside this class');\n    }\n}\n\nconst secure = new Secure();\n\nfunction callPrivateMethod() {\n    secure.#privateMethod();\n}\n\ncallPrivateMethod();\n```"
