"```python\n# No Python code is required to solve this problem as it's a SwiftUI question.\n```\n\nHowever, if you want to achieve similar functionality in a Python application using a library like Tkinter or PyQt, you would need to create a custom widget that can detect when a date picker is opened or closed. Here's an example using Tkinter:\n\n```python\nimport tkinter as tk\nfrom tkinter import ttk\n\nclass DatePicker(tk.Frame):\n    def __init__(self, master, **kwargs):\n        super().__init__(master, **kwargs)\n        self.is_open = False\n        self.date = None\n        self.parent = master\n        self.config(bg=\"white\")\n        self.create_widgets()\n\n    def create_widgets(self):\n        self.date_picker = ttk.Combobox(self, values=[str(i) for i in range(2024)])\n        self.date_picker.current(0)\n        self.date_picker.bind(\"<Button-1>\", self.on_date_picker_click)\n        self.date_picker.bind(\"<KeyRelease>\", self.on_date_picker_key_release)\n        self.date_picker.bind(\"<KeyRelease-Return>\", self.on_date_picker_return)\n        self.date_picker.bind(\"<KeyRelease-Escape>\", self.on_date_picker_escape)\n\n    def on_date_picker_click(self, event):\n        self.is_open = True\n        self.parent.config(bg=\"gray\")\n        self.date = self.date_picker.get()\n\n    def on_date_picker_key_release(self, event):\n        if event.keysym == \"Escape\":\n            self.is_open = False\n            self.parent.config(bg=\"white\")\n            self.date = None\n\n    def on_date_picker_return(self, event):\n        if event.keysym == \"Return\":\n            self.is_open = False\n            self.parent.config(bg=\"white\")\n            self.date = self.date_picker.get()\n\n    def on_date_picker_escape(self, event):\n        if event.keysym == \"Escape\":\n            self.is_open = False\n            self.parent.config(bg=\"white\")\n            self.date = None\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.title(\"DatePicker Example\")\ndate_picker = DatePicker(root)\ndate_picker.pack(padx=10, pady=10)\nroot.mainloop()\n```\n\nThis example creates a custom `DatePicker` widget that can be used in a Tkinter application. The widget has a `Combobox` that displays a list of dates, and it can be opened and closed using the mouse or keyboard. When the date picker is opened, the parent window's background color changes to gray. When the date picker is closed, the parent window's background color returns to white."
