"class GitBearerAuth:\n    def __init__(self, pat):\n        self.pat = pat\n\n    def __call__(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        kwargs['http_headers'] = {'Authorization': f'Bearer {self.pat}'}\n        return kwargs\n\ndef gitBearerAuth(pat):\n    return GitBearerAuth(pat)\n\ndef git_clone_withBearer(url, pat):\n    return git -c gitBearerAuth(pat) clone url\n\n# Usage:\npat = \"your_pat_here\"\nurl = \"https://bitbucketserver.com/scm/projectname/teamsinspace.git\"\ngit_clone_withBearer(url, pat)assistant\n\nThis code defines a class `GitBearerAuth` that can be used to override the HTTP `Authorization` header with a Bearer token. The `__call__` method is used to make the class callable, allowing it to be used as a function.\n\nThe `gitBearerAuth` function returns an instance of `GitBearerAuth` with the given PAT.\n\nThe `git_clone_withBearer` function uses `git` to clone a repository with the Bearer token authentication.\n\nYou can replace `\"your_pat_here\"` with your actual PAT and `url` with the URL of the repository you want to clone."
