"```python\nimport subprocess\n\ndef get_face(image_path):\n    # Load the native library\n    subprocess.run(['ld', '-r', '-o', 'libopencv_java480.so', 'opencv-4.8.0/opencv-4.8.0/solib/libopencv_java480.so'])\n\n    # Create a cascade classifier\n    cascade_classifier = cv2.CascadeClassifier('/opt/docker/opencv-4.8.0/data/haarcascades/haarcascade_frontalface_default.xml')\n\n    # Read the image\n    image = cv2.imread(image_path)\n\n    # Convert the image to grayscale\n    gray = cv2.cvtColor(image, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)\n\n    # Detect faces in the image\n    faces = cascade_classifier.detectMultiScale(gray)\n\n    # If faces are detected\n    if len(faces) > 0:\n        # Get the first face\n        face_rect = faces[0]\n\n        # Calculate the padding\n        padding = 220\n        start_y = max(0, face_rect[1] - padding)\n        end_y = min(image.shape[0], face_rect[1] + face_rect[3] + padding)\n        start_x = max(0, face_rect[0])\n        end_x = min(image.shape[1], face_rect[0] + face_rect[2])\n\n        # Return the face\n        return image[start_y:end_y, start_x:end_x]\n    else:\n        # Return an empty image\n        return None\n\nimage_path = '/src/main/resources/MyImage.png'\nface = get_face(image_path)\nif face is not None:\n    cv2.imshow('Face', face)\n    cv2.waitKey(0)\n    cv2.destroyAllWindows()\n```\n\nThis code should be placed in a `.py` file and run inside the Docker container. It loads the native library, creates a cascade classifier, reads the image, converts it to grayscale, detects faces, and returns the face if detected.\n\nPlease note that you need to have OpenCV installed in the Docker container. You have already installed it in the Dockerfile using the following command:\n\n```bash\ndockerCommands += Seq(\n  Cmd(\"RUN\", \"apk\", \"add\", \"--no-cache\", \"bash make cmake g++ wget unzip\"),\n  Cmd(\"RUN\", \"apk\", \"add\", \"--no-cache\", \"linux-headers\"),\n  Cmd(\"RUN\", \"wget -O opencv.zip https://github.com/opencv/opencv/archive/4.8.0.zip\"),\n  Cmd(\"RUN\", \"unzip opencv.zip && rm opencv.zip\"),\n  Cmd(\"RUN\", \"mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake ../opencv-4.8.0 && make -j4 && make install\")\n)\n```\n\nAlso, you need to mount the `opencv4` directory from the host machine to the Docker container. This is done in the `docker-compose.yml` file:\n\n```yml\nversion: '3'\nservices:\n  scala-service:\n    image: human-verification-test:0.0.1\n    volumes:\n      - ./src/main/resources:/src/main/resources\n      - ./opencv4:/opt/docker/opencv-4.8.0\n    ports:\n      - \"8083:8083\"\n```\n\nThis way, the `opencv4` directory is accessible from the Docker container and you can use the `haarcascade_frontalface_default.xml` file in your code."
"```python\nimport cv2\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef remove_vertical_streaks(image_path):\n    # Read the image\n    image = cv2.imread(image_path)\n\n    # Convert the image to grayscale\n    gray = cv2.cvtColor(image, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)\n\n    # Apply Canny edge detection\n    edges = cv2.Canny(gray, 50, 150)\n\n    # Find the contours of the vertical streaks\n    contours, _ = cv2.findContours(edges, cv2.RETR_EXTERNAL, cv2.CHAIN_APPROX_SIMPLE)\n\n    # Iterate through the contours and remove the streaks\n    for contour in contours:\n        area = cv2.contourArea(contour)\n        x, y, w, h = cv2.boundingRect(contour)\n        aspect_ratio = float(w)/h\n        if area > 100 and aspect_ratio > 5 and aspect_ratio < 10:\n            cv2.drawContours(image, [contour], 0, (0, 0, 0), -1)\n            cv2.fillPoly(image, [contour], (0, 0, 0))\n\n    # Return the image with vertical streaks removed\n    return image\n\n# Example usage\nimage_path = 'iamHP.jpg'\noutput = remove_vertical_streaks(image_path)\ncv2.imshow('Output', output)\ncv2.waitKey(0)\ncv2.destroyAllWindows()\n```"
"```python\nimport cv2\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Load the image\nimg = cv2.imread('input_distorted.jpg')\n\n# Convert the image to grayscale\ngray = cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)\n\n# Calculate the coefficients of the lens distortion\ncoeffs = np.polyfit([x for x, y in zip(gray.shape[1], gray[:, 0])], [y for y, x in zip(gray[:, 0], gray[:, 0])], 2)\n\n# Define the polynomial function to correct the distortion\ndef correct_distortion(x):\n    return coeffs[0]*x**2 + coeffs[1]*x + coeffs[2]\n\n# Correct the distortion in each row of the image\ncorrected_img = np.zeros_like(gray)\nfor y in range(gray.shape[0]):\n    for x in range(gray.shape[1]):\n        corrected_img[y, x] = correct_distortion(x)\n\n# Convert the corrected image back to BGR\ncorrected_img = cv2.cvtColor(corrected_img, cv2.COLOR_GRAY2BGR)\n\n# Save the corrected image\ncv2.imwrite('input_distorted_corrected.jpg', corrected_img)\n```"
