"```python\nfrom dateutil import parser\nimport yup\n\ndef validate_date(date_str, date_format):\n    try:\n        date = parser.parse(date_str, dayfirst=True)\n        if date_format == 'dd.MM.yyyy':\n            return date.strftime('%Y-%m-%d')\n        elif date_format == 'yyyy/MM/dd':\n            return date.strftime('%Y-%m-%d')\n        else:\n            return date_str\n    except ValueError:\n        return False\n\ndate_to_check = yup\n    .date()\n    .nullable()\n    .transform(lambda curr, orig: (orig === '' ? None : curr))\n    .required()\n    .min('2000-01-01', 'Date cannot be this early')\n    .max('9999-12-31', 'Date too much in the future')\n    .test('format', 'Date is invalid', lambda date: validate_date(date, 'dd.MM.yyyy'))\n```\n\nNote: This code will validate the date in 'dd.MM.yyyy' format and return False if it's not. You can change the date format in the `validate_date` function to match your desired format."
"```python\n# Define a custom validator for the Title field\nclass TitleFieldValidator:\n    def validate(self, value, field) -> None:\n        if len(value) > 50:\n            raise ValidationFailure(field, 'Title is too long. Maximal length is 50 characters, but actual is $value')\n        elif len(value) < 10:\n            raise ValidationFailure(field, 'Title is too short. Minimal length is 10 characters, but actual is $value')\n\n# Use the custom validator in your class\nclass MyEntity {\n  @IsString()\n  @IsLength({ min: 10, max: 50 })\n  @TitleFieldValidator()\n  title: string;\n}\n```"
"```python\n# Python equivalent of Ruby's keyword arguments in function calls\ndef validate_each(record, attribute, value, **options):\n    postcode = UKPostcode.parse(value.to_s)\n    return if postcode.full_valid()\n\n    record.errors.add(attribute, :invalid_uk_postcode, options)\n```\n\nThe issue in Ruby 3.0 is that the `record.errors.add` method expects either no arguments or at most two. However, when you pass three arguments (the attribute, the error message, and the options), it throws an `ArgumentError`.\n\nIn Python, the `**options` syntax allows you to pass any number of keyword arguments to a function. This is similar to how Ruby's `**options` syntax works in the original code. By using `**options` in the function definition, you're making the `options` parameter a variable-length argument list, which allows you to pass any number of keyword arguments to the function.\n\nThis change is equivalent to Ruby's `options` being keyword arguments, which can take on any type and value. The `**options` syntax is a Pythonic way of achieving the same effect as Ruby's `options` being keyword arguments."
"```python\nimport subprocess\n\ndef validate_yaml(file_path):\n    try:\n        # Run yq with the -v option and redirect output to /dev/null\n        output = subprocess.check_output(['yq', '-v', file_path], stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)\n        return None  # Return None if the file is valid\n    except subprocess.CalledProcessError as e:\n        # If yq exits with a non-zero status, print the error message\n        print(f\"Error validating YAML file: {e}\")\n        return f\"Error validating YAML file: {e}\"\n\n# Example usage\nfile_path = './file.yml'\nresult = validate_yaml(file_path)\nprint(result)\n```"
