"```python\nimport socket\n\ndef send_synack_tcp(ip, port):\n    sock = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)\n    sock.setsockopt(socket.IPPROTO_IP, socket.IP_DONTFRAG, 1)\n    sock.connect((ip, port))\n    sock.send(b\"SYN\")\n    sock.send(b\"ACK\")\n    sock.close()\n\ndef receive_synack_tcp(ip, port):\n    sock = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)\n    sock.setsockopt(socket.IPPROTO_IP, socket.IP_DONTFRAG, 1)\n    sock.connect((ip, port))\n    data, addr = sock.recvfrom(1024)\n    print(f\"Received SYN-ACK packet: {data.decode('utf-8')}\")\n    sock.close()\n\ndef main():\n    ip = \"127.0.0.1\"\n    port = 2222\n    send_synack_tcp(ip, port)\n    receive_synack_tcp(ip, port)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nExplanation:\nThe problem in your Go program is that you're not properly setting up the SYN+ACK packet. The SYN packet is sent first, followed by the ACK packet from the server. However, in your code, you're sending the SYN+ACK packet directly without waiting for the server's response.\n\nTo fix this, you need to wait for the server's SYN+ACK packet before sending your own ACK packet. This is why your server is replying with RST (Reset) - it's not receiving the expected ACK packet from your client.\n\nIn the modified Python code, we first send a SYN packet to the server, then we wait for the server's SYN+ACK packet using the `recvfrom` method. After receiving the packet, we send our own ACK packet to the server."
"```javascript\nimport express, { Request, Response } from 'express';\nimport cors from 'cors';\nimport { Configuration, OpenAIAPI } from 'openai';\nimport io from 'socket.io';\n\n// ...\n\nconst app = express();\nconst server = require('http').createServer(app);\nconst ioServer = io(server);\n\napp.post('/api/admin/testStream', async (req: Request, res: Response) => {\n    const { password } = req.body;\n\n    try {\n        if (password !== process.env.ADMIN_PASSWORD) {\n            res.send({ message: 'Incorrect password' });\n            return;\n        }\n\n        const openai = new OpenAIAPI(new Configuration({\n            api_key: process.env.OPENAI_API_KEY,\n        }));\n\n        const completion = await openai.createCompletion({\n            model: 'text-davinci-003',\n            prompt: 'Say this is a test',\n            stream: true,\n        }, { responseType: 'stream' });\n\n        completion.data.on('data', (chunk: any) => {\n            ioServer.emit('streamData', chunk.toString());\n        });\n\n        res.send({ message: 'Stream started' });\n    } catch (err) {\n        console.log(err);\n        res.send(err);\n    }\n});\n\n// ...\n\nserver.listen(3000, () => {\n    console.log('Server is listening on port 3000');\n});\n```\n\nThis code sets up an Express.js server, creates a Socket.IO server, and listens for incoming requests on port 3000. When the `/api/admin/testStream` endpoint is called, it creates a completion using OpenAI and emits a `streamData` event to all connected clients with the received chunks. \n\nOn the frontend, you can use Socket.IO to receive the chunks and update the UI accordingly."
