"```python\nclass WarehouseSum:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.wh_units = {}\n\n    def available_units(self, warehouse: str) -> int:\n        if warehouse not in self.wh_units:\n            # Simulate the SQL query\n            total_units = 100  # Replace with actual database query\n            self.wh_units[warehouse] = total_units\n        return self.wh_units[warehouse]\n\n# Example usage:\nwarehouse_sum = WarehouseSum()\nprint(warehouse_sum.available_units(\"Cohasset\"))\nprint(warehouse_sum.available_units(\"Cohasset\"))\n```\n\nThe issue in your code lies in how you're checking if `wh_units` is `Nothing` and how you're adding new entries to it. In VBA, `Nothing` is used to represent an object that has not been initialized or has been set to `Nothing`. However, when you try to access an object that is `Nothing`, you'll get a runtime error.\n\nIn your case, the problem is that you're setting `wh_units` to `Nothing` when the warehouse is not in the dictionary, but then you're not re-checking if `wh_units` is `Nothing` before trying to access it again. This is why you're getting a runtime error when you try to access `wh_units` for the second time.\n\nTo fix this issue, you should re-check if `wh_units` is `Nothing` after setting it to a new dictionary. If it is `Nothing`, you should create a new dictionary; otherwise, you can use the existing one.\n\nIn Python, we don't need to explicitly check if an object is `None` like you would in VBA, because `None` is a built-in singleton in Python that represents the absence of a value. So, we can simply initialize `wh_units` as an empty dictionary and use it directly."
"```python\nclass ReferenceObject:\n    def __init__(self, obj):\n        self.obj = obj\n\nclass S4Class(ReferenceObject):\n    def __init__(self, obj):\n        super().__init__(obj)\n\n# Usage:\neeg = eeg_class()  # assuming eeg_class is your S4 class\nanalysis = S4Class(eeg)\n```\n\nIn R, you can achieve this by using the `ReferenceClass` package which provides a way to create classes that store a reference to an object instead of duplicating it. Here's an example of how you can do it:\n\n```r\n# Load the ReferenceClass package\nlibrary(ReferenceClass)\n\n# Define the EEG class\neeg <- setRefClass(\n  \"EEG\",\n  fields = list(data = \"numeric\"),\n  methods = list(\n    \"analyze\", \n    # other methods...\n  )\n)\n\n# Define the Analysis class that stores a reference to the EEG object\nanalysis <- setRefClass(\n  \"Analysis\",\n  fields = list(eeg = \"Reference(EEG)\"),\n  methods = list(\n    \"analyze\", \n    # methods that require the original EEG data...\n  )\n)\n\n# Create an instance of EEG and Analysis\neeg_obj <- new(\"EEG\", data = runif(10000))\nanalysis_obj <- new(\"Analysis\", eeg = eeg_obj)\n```"
