"```python\nfrom typing import List, Optional\n\nclass Pill:\n    def __init__(self, label: str):\n        self.label = label\n\nclass PillToggle(ContentView):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self._pills = ObservableCollection[Pill]()\n        self.pills = property(lambda self: self._pills)\n\n    def initialize(self):\n        self._pills = ObservableCollection[Pill]()\n        self.pills = property(lambda self: self._pills)\n        BindingContext.SetBinding(self, 'BindingContext', self, source=self)\n        BindableLayout.SetItemsSource(self.PillsContainer, self.pills)\n\n    @property\n    def PillsProperty(self) -> BindableProperty:\n        return BindableProperty.Create('Pills', type(self._pills), type(self), default=self._pills)\n\n    @PillsProperty.setter\n    def PillsProperty(self, value: ObservableCollection[Pill]):\n        self._pills = value\n```\n\nThe issue with your code is that you're not calling the `Initialize` method which sets up the binding in the `__init__` method. In the `Initialize` method, you're also setting the `BindingContext` to the view model which is not necessary.\n\nIn the `MyPage.xaml` file, you should use the `PillsProperty` setter instead of the `Pills` property.\n\n```xml\n<controls:PillToggle x:Name=\"MyToggle\" Pills=\"{Binding MyPills}\">\n    <controls:PillToggle.Pills>\n        <controls:Pill Label=\"Test 3\" Selected=\"True\"/>\n        <controls:Pill Label=\"Test 4\"/>\n    </controls:PillToggle.Pills>\n</controls:PillToggle>\n```\n\nNote that I've also added a `PillsProperty` setter in the `PillToggle` class to match the usage in the XAML file."
