"```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <memory>\n\nstruct Parent {\n    Parent() {\n        std::cout << \"Parent created\" << std::endl;\n    }\n\n    ~Parent() {\n        std::cout << \"Parent destroyed\" << std::endl;\n    }\n};\n\nstruct Child : public Parent {\n    Child() {\n        std::cout << \"Child created\" << std::endl;\n    }\n\n    ~Child() {\n        std::cout << \"Child destroyed\" << std::endl;\n    }\n\n    std::unique_ptr<Parent> content;\n};\n\nstruct SecondChild : public Parent {\n    SecondChild() {\n        std::cout << \"SecondChild created\" << std::endl;\n    }\n\n    ~SecondChild() {\n        std::cout << \"SecondChild destroyed\" << std::endl;\n    }\n\n    int val;\n};\n\nstd::unique_ptr<Parent> foo() {\n    auto test = std::make_unique<Child>();\n    auto content_in_child = std::make_unique<SecondChild>();\n    content_in_child->val = 4;\n    test->content = std::move(content_in_child);\n    return test;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::unique_ptr<Parent> high = foo();\n    while (high) {\n        high.reset();\n        high = std::move(high);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nThe issue in your code lies in the way you handle the ownership of the `SecondChild` object. When you assign the `content_in_child` pointer to `test->content`, you are not transferring ownership of `content_in_child`, you are just assigning a pointer. This means that `test` still holds a reference to `content_in_child`, and `content_in_child` is not destroyed when `test` goes out of scope.\n\nTo fix this issue, you need to transfer ownership of `content_in_child` to `test`. You can do this by using `std::move` when assigning `content_in_child` to `test->content`. However, this will not work in your case because `content_in_child` is a local variable and will be destroyed when `foo` returns.\n\nOne way to solve this issue is to change the ownership of `content_in_child` to `test` by using a shared pointer. However, since you want to keep the polymorphic behavior, you can use a technique called \"smart pointer composition\" to transfer ownership of `content_in_child` to `test` without changing the ownership of `Parent` objects in `Child`. \n\nIn the refactored code, I've added a `while` loop in `main` to ensure that all objects are properly destroyed."
