"```python\nclass Meta(type):\n    def __new__(cls, name, bases, dct):\n        new_class = type(name, bases, dct)\n        new_class.attr = 100  # add some to class\n        return new_class\n\n\nclass WithAttr(metaclass=Meta):\n    pass\n\n\nprint(type(WithAttr))  # Output: <class 'type'>\n```\n\nThe code prints `<class 'type'>` instead of `<class '__main__.Meta'>` because in Python, when you define a class using a metaclass, the class itself is actually created by the metaclass. Therefore, `type(WithAttr)` returns the type of the class created by `WithAttr`, which is the built-in `type` class. The metaclass `Meta` is not directly accessible through `type(WithAttr)`.\n\nYes, you are correct that `WithAttr` is an instance of `Meta`. However, it's more accurate to say that `WithAttr` is a subclass of `Meta`, because `Meta` is the metaclass used to create `WithAttr`."
