"```python\n# The issue here is not with the Python code itself, but rather with JavaScript.\n# The problem is with the use of private methods and fields in JavaScript.\n\nclass Base:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._public()\n\n    def _public(self):\n        self._private_method()\n\n    def _private_method(self):\n        self.bar = 1234\n\nclass Child(Base):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n\nchild = Child()\nprint(child.bar)\n```\n\nThe issue in JavaScript is that private methods and fields are not directly accessible from the constructor of a subclass. The `super()` function is used to call methods of the parent class, but it doesn't allow direct access to private methods and fields.\n\nThe error message you're seeing is because the JavaScript interpreter is trying to access the private method `_privateMethod` from the `Child` class, but it's not allowed because it's not an instance of the `Child` class. Instead, it's an instance of the `Base` class, which doesn't have the same private methods and fields.\n\nThe `#` symbol in front of a method or property name in JavaScript makes it private, but it's not enforced by the JavaScript engine. It's more like a convention to indicate that the method or property should not be accessed directly from outside the class.\n\nIn your case, you're trying to access the private method `_privateMethod` from the constructor of the `Child` class, which is not allowed. To fix this issue, you can add a public method to the `Child` class that calls the private method, or you can remove the private method and access it directly from the constructor."
