"# Fine-Grained Permissions for GitHub Personal Tokens\n\n## Understanding the Issue\n\nWhen creating a fine-grained personal access token, you didn't select any user permissions or repository permissions. This could have led to the 403 error you encountered while trying to push to a repository.\n\n## Recommended Permissions\n\nTo avoid similar issues in the future, choose the following permissions when creating a fine-grained personal access token:\n\n*   **repo**: This permission allows you to read and write to public repositories.\n*   **read:repo**: This permission allows you to read public repositories, but not write to them.\n*   **write:repo**: This permission allows you to write to public repositories, but not read from them.\n\nAvoid selecting the following permissions, as they may cause issues:\n\n*   **admin**: This permission grants you full control over a repository and all its contents.\n*   **maint**: This permission allows you to push updates to a repository, but not delete it.\n*   **delete_repo**: This permission allows you to delete a repository.\n\n## Example Code\n\nHere's an example of how you can use a fine-grained personal access token with the recommended permissions:\n\n```bash\n# Set the token and repository URL\nTOKEN=\"your_fine_grained_token\"\nREPOSITORY_URL=\"https://github.com/Username/X.git\"\n\n# Use the token to authenticate with GitHub\ngit config --global user.name \"Your Name\"\ngit config --global user.email \"your_email@example.com\"\n\n# Add the repository as a remote\ngit remote add origin \"$REPOSITORY_URL\"\n\n# Fetch the repository\ngit fetch origin\n\n# Push the changes\ngit push -u origin main\n```\n\nRemember to replace `your_fine_grained_token` with your actual personal access token and `Your Name` and `your_email@example.com` with your desired GitHub username and email address."
